# colony.gen -- a line proposes, a colony disposes, a memory harmonizes it.
#
# Four stages in one chain, and each one only hears the one before it.
# That is the whole idea: a chain is a pipeline, so a piece can be built
# out of small honest transformations of a signal rather than out of one
# clever generator. Read it left to right on the canvas.
#
#   gen::euclid       proposes   a Euclidean rhythm, one bar long
#   gen::life         disposes   the line draws on Conway's board and is
#                                eaten; what you hear is what grew
#   gen::markov       remembers  learns the colony's transitions and
#                                paraphrases them alongside it
#   xform::harmonize  answers    every note becomes a chord spelled from
#                                the scale, not from fixed intervals
#
# THE LINE IS NEVER HEARD. `pass = 0' on the board, which is the version
# of this worth shipping: the line's notes are turned into *cells* and
# stop there, so every pitch that reaches your ears is one Conway put
# back. The line is a hand reaching into the board while it runs, and the
# hand is the piece rather than a person -- gen::life has taken clicks
# since glider.gen, and a note arriving from upstream is the same edit
# with nobody's mouse in it.
#
# HOW A NOTE BECOMES A CELL. The board's tick reads row y as a degree on
# the ladder and column x as a time within the generation. Receiving runs
# both mappings backwards: the pitch picks the row, the arrival time
# picks the column. So a phrase draws its own shape in pitch and its own
# rhythm in time, and then B3/S23 argues with it. A pitch the ladder
# cannot spell has no row and is dropped -- put a gen::quantize upstream
# if you would rather every note landed. That is the piece's decision to
# state, not the plugin's to assume.
#
# WHY A RHYTHM AND NOT A TAPE LOOP. Because one cell on its own dies.
# Conway needs three neighbours to make anything, so a sparse line that
# drops a single cell every few seconds is a line whose cells are all
# dead before the next one lands -- it can perturb the board but it can
# never build on it. A euclid draws a *figure*: sixteen steps at 0.28s
# is one bar of 4.48s, which is exactly one generation, so each bar lays
# seven onsets into sixteen columns and the pitch pool walks the rows
# as it cycles. That is a diagonal scattered across the board, redrawn
# every generation, and diagonals are what Conway makes things out of.
#
# The difference is not subtle and is worth hearing for yourself. Set
# `fills = 0' and the board plays three pitches forever -- the blinker,
# alone, a metronome, with markov paraphrasing a metronome. Put the line
# back and the colony spreads over all eight rows inside a minute and
# more than doubles what the piece plays. The line is silent throughout;
# every note of that difference is a consequence.
#
# WHY THE BOARD STARTS ALMOST EMPTY. One blinker, three cells, a
# two-generation cycle. It gives the piece a pulse from the first bar and
# leaves the rest of the board for the line to fill, so what you hear
# early is mostly the line's drawing and what you hear later is mostly
# the collisions. Double-click the `board' stage on the canvas to watch
# it; you can still click cells yourself while it plays, and the line
# will draw around whatever you leave.
#
# `wrap = 0', so the board has edges. On a torus the colony has nowhere
# to lose anything and saturates into a soup inside two minutes; with
# edges, things drift off and are gone, and the piece keeps a density it
# can hold. A piece is a shape over time, and a board that fills up has
# stopped having one.
#
# WHY THE HARMONY IS DIATONIC. xform::harmonize counts scale degrees, not
# semitones. A harmonizer that added a fixed interval would be a pitch
# shifter -- the same chord everywhere, the line moved sideways. Counting
# degrees means the chord's quality comes from where in the scale the
# melody is, which is the difference between harmony and processing. The
# scale here is pentatonic, so two degrees up is a fourth and four is a
# seventh: every stack is open and nothing can clash.
#
# THE INSTRUMENT MOVES TOO. Underneath, phase 4's two structure edits:
# `gen::swap' rebuilds the pad's channel around a different instrument
# every forty-five seconds, and `gen::reshape' reaches inside whatever is
# there and sweeps a constant the patch never declared. The notes above
# know nothing about either. See reshape.gen for the argument; this piece
# is that argument put underneath something with a lot going on, which is
# where you can actually hear what it buys.
#
# TURN THE DENSITY KNOB while it plays. It sets how many voices each
# chord has, from one -- the bare colony, no harmony at all -- to five.
# It is the fastest way to hear which part of this is Conway and which
# part is the harmonizer.
#
# Self-contained: it carries its own instruments, so open it and press
# Play.

name "Colony";
author "Misha Nasledov";
description "A line seeds Conway's board; what grows is learned and harmonized.";

seed 3391;

# A minor pentatonic over two octaves. Eight notes for eight rows, so
# every row of the board has a pitch and every pitch the line can play
# has a row -- the two ends of the same ladder, which is what makes the
# line able to draw at all.
scale pent "A2 C3 D3 E3 G3 A3 C4 D4";

# The low end, for the chain that goes the other way.
scale under "A1 C2 E2 A2";

@density = 2;
@density.min = 1;
@density.max = 5;

instrument pad {
    dsp  "amb01.dsp";
    a    = 260 ms;
    d    = 1400 ms;
    s    = 0.62;
    r    = 2400 ms;
    res  = 0.55;
    fmin = 0.1;
    fmax = 0.6;
};

instrument bell {
    dsp  "amb01.dsp";
    a    = 6 ms;
    d    = 2400 ms;
    s    = 0.08;
    r    = 2200 ms;
    res  = 0.88;
    fmin = 0.25;
    fmax = 0.95;
};

instrument wood {
    dsp  "ts1.dsp";
    a    = 30 ms;
    d    = 700 ms;
    s    = 0.35;
    r    = 1500 ms;
    cutoff = 7;
    res    = 0.45;
};

instrument bass {
    dsp  "amb01.dsp";
    a    = 900 ms;
    d    = 2600 ms;
    s    = 0.8;
    r    = 4000 ms;
    res  = 0.3;
    fmin = 0.04;
    fmax = 0.22;
};

# The chain the piece is about.
#
# The line's bar and the board's generation are the same length on
# purpose: sixteen steps of 0.28s is 4.48s, and so is the generation.
# One onset per column, one bar per generation, no drift, so the figure
# lands on the same cells every bar and reinforces itself.
#
# Change either number without the other and the piece does not blur, it
# stops: the figure walks sideways a little each generation, never draws
# the same cell twice running, and every cell it draws is alone and dead
# before the next bar. Measured on a scratch board, an aligned line gave
# 136 notes where an unaligned one gave 1. Alignment is not a nicety
# here; it is the difference between drawing and scribbling.
chain colony {
    stage line gen::euclid {
        steps = 16;  fills = 7;  rotate = 0;
        notes = pent;
        period = 0.28 s;  hold = 0.3 s;  vel = 72;
    };

    # One blinker, and nothing else. Rows are written top-down and
    # stored bottom-up, so this sits high on the ladder:
    #
    #   ................
    #   ................
    #   ...OOO..........
    #   ................
    #   ................
    #   ................
    #   ................
    #   ................
    stage board gen::life {
        width = 16;  height = 8;  wrap = 0;  scatter = 0;
        board = "................/................/...OOO........../................/................/................/................/................";
        notes = pent;
        trigger = 0;
        period = 4.48 s;  hold = 3.6 s;  vel = 64;
        listen = 1;
        pass = 0;
    };

    # Order 2, so it learns pairs rather than single steps -- a colony
    # produces chords and runs, and an order-1 table flattens both into
    # a cloud. `pass = 1' keeps the board audible underneath the dream,
    # which matters here: with it off you would be listening to a
    # paraphrase of something you never heard.
    stage dream gen::markov {
        order = 2;  pass = 1;
        period = 2.2 s;  hold = 3 s;  vel = 54;
    };

    # And the chord. `spread' rolls it, which keeps a dense passage
    # legible: five voices arriving together is a block, five arriving
    # 90ms apart is a gesture.
    stage chord xform::harmonize {
        scale = pent;
        voices = @density;
        step = 2;
        spread = 0.09 s;
        taper = 0.82;
        root = 1;
        below = 0;
    };

    sink { instrument = pad; };
};

# The other direction, so the piece has a floor.
#
# `below = 1' stacks *downward*: the note the line plays becomes the top
# of its own harmony instead of the bottom. Two voices, very slow, on its
# own instrument -- the same plugin as the chain above, aimed the other
# way, which is the cheapest possible demonstration that the direction is
# a param and not a second plugin.
chain floor {
    stage low gen::eno_line {
        notes = under;
        period = 11 s;  jitter = 3 s;  prob = 0.7;
        hold = 13 s;  vel = 48;  vel_jitter = 4;
    };

    stage chord xform::harmonize {
        scale = under;
        voices = 2;  step = 2;
        spread = 1.4 s;
        taper = 0.9;
        below = 1;
    };

    sink { instrument = bass; };
};

# The instrument under the colony is rebuilt every forty-five seconds.
# Round-robin, so the piece has a shape you can learn; the swap service
# does nothing at all when the list names what is already there, so the
# first tick is not a rebuild into a copy of itself.
chain swapping {
    stage m gen::swap {
        instruments = "pad,bell,wood";
        every = 45 s;
        order = 0;
    };
    sink { instrument = pad; };
};

# And the fine edit. `fmap' is the node inside amb01.dsp that maps a
# note's velocity onto the filter's range; `inmax' is the top of the
# velocity it expects, and it is not a chanarg -- amb01 never offered it.
# Lowering it makes a soft note open the filter as far as a hard one, so
# the harmonizer's tapered voices stop sitting under the melody and come
# up level with it. The taper is still there; the instrument has stopped
# caring about it.
#
# Aimed at the slot, so when the swap puts ts1.dsp there the node is gone
# and the host says so once per step rather than pretending. Nothing else
# in the piece stops.
chain sensitivity {
    stage r gen::reshape {
        node = "fmap";  arg = "inmax";
        from = 1;  to = 0.3;
        every = 13 s;  steps = 7;
    };
    sink { instrument = pad; };
};
